id: k8s.resources.requests
kind: kubernetes
title: Resource requests
aliases: requests, cpu request, memory request
tags: resources, scheduling
questions:
  - what do CPU and memory requests actually do?
  - a pod has been Pending for ten minutes — how do you find out why?
invariant: Requests are what the scheduler reserves for placement and what QoS is computed from.
answer_30s: Requests are the guaranteed capacity the scheduler reserves for a Pod, and the basis for QoS class and eviction order.
answer_2m: |
  The scheduler places a Pod only where the sum of the requests of what already
  runs leaves room for this Pod's requests -- so an over-stated request makes a
  cluster look full, and an under-stated one lets the node be oversubscribed.
  Kubernetes schedules on requests, not on usage. requests == limits gives the
  Guaranteed QoS class, which is the most protected profile during node
  pressure.
when_to_use:
  - every container, always; budgets are what make HPA and eviction predictable
commands:
  - kubectl describe node <node> => allocated vs allocatable CPU/memory, i.e. why a Pod stays Pending
  - kubectl get pod api-... -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].resources}' => declared requests/limits
failure_modes:
  - requests summed above node allocatable, so Pods are permanently Pending
  - only limits set, so the scheduler plans with a default request and oversubscribes
related:
  - k8s.resources.limits
  - k8s.scheduler
  - k8s.hpa
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/
